@page "/learn/wasm-culture-from-server"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>The same culture in the browser · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">The same culture in the browser</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">The server renders a German invoice. Does it stay German once WebAssembly takes over?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a prerendered WebAssembly component now starts in the culture the server
        rendered it in. .NET 10 could get there too, but only if you passed the culture along yourself, from a
        start-up script or into each component. This page shows what went wrong without that, then what changed.
    </p>

    <p>
        A German customer opens an invoice. The page is rendered on the server, in the request's culture, so the
        total reads <code>1.234,56</code> and the due date reads <i>Freitag</i>. The invoice itself is a WebAssembly
        component: it's prerendered with the page, then runs in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        The browser has its own language, and it may not be German. .NET 11 changed how WebAssembly starts, so the
        old behaviour can't run live here. Fig. 1 replays two moments recorded from a real .NET 10 app, in an
        English browser.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The invoice on .NET 10" Url="demo/wasm-culture/invoice/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            This replays what .NET 10 did; nothing in it runs live. Read the first two rows. The server sent
            <b>1.234,56</b> and <b>Freitag</b>. Once WebAssembly started, the same component showed <b>1,234.56</b>
            and <b>Friday</b>: the browser's language won. The third row passed the culture in as a parameter. Its
            total stayed German, but the date, the one format call that forgot it, still flipped. Its Culture column
            still reads <b>en-US</b> because the parameter only reaches the calls it's handed to; the runtime's own
            culture never changed.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Two renders, two cultures</h2>
    <p>A prerendered WebAssembly component renders twice, and in .NET 10 each render picked its culture on its own:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where each render got its culture in .NET 10</span>
        <pre class="snip">1. Server prerender   culture from the request <span class="dim">(?culture=de-DE)</span>   <span class="ok">1.234,56</span>
2. WebAssembly starts culture from the browser <span class="dim">(navigator.languages[0])</span>  <span class="err">1,234.56</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The server's culture never reached the browser. So the same <code>ToString("N2")</code> gave a German answer
        on the server and an English one a moment later. In a French browser, the repro showed
        <code>1 234,56</code> and <i>vendredi</i>: whatever the browser speaks, that's what you get.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Register localization, and keep prerendering on</h2>
    <p>
        There's no new API to call. A localized app already registers localization in the server's
        <code>Program.cs</code>, and WebAssembly components prerender by default. On .NET 11 those two together are
        the fix:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What turns it on</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// Program.cs (server)</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddLocalization();</span>
...
app.UseRequestLocalization(...);

<span class="dim">// The page: the component must be prerendered (the default)</span>
&lt;InvoiceSummary <span class="hl">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly"</span> /&gt;
<span class="dim">// not new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false): no prerender, no culture</span>

<span class="dim">// The component: plain formatting, no culture passed in</span>
&lt;td&gt;@@Total.ToString("N2")&lt;/td&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Upgrade, keep the prerender, and the component's formatting needs nothing extra. Here's an invoice on .NET 11,
        rendered in German.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="An invoice on .NET 11" Url="demo/wasm-culture/invoice?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Wait for the badge to say <b>Running on WebAssembly</b>. It says <b>Formatting in de-DE</b>, whatever your
            browser's language. The table at the bottom keeps what the server rendered: <b>Server rendered</b> and
            <b>WebAssembly now</b> match. Then pick <b>fr-FR</b>. The page loads in full, and WebAssembly starts in
            French. This is a live order from the demo database, so its total and due date aren't the recording's;
            the amounts are US dollars formatted as plain numbers (<code>N2</code>, no currency sign), which is why
            they read German-style.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Nothing in the demo's code mentions the browser's culture. Here are its three files:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/WasmCulture/Invoice.razor" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            The page Fig. 2 runs. Request localization sets its culture from <code>?culture=</code>, and it prerenders
            the invoice component with <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/WasmCulture/InvoiceSummary.razor" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            The component itself. It formats with plain <code>ToString("N2")</code> and <code>ToString("D")</code>,
            the same code that flipped in Fig. 1, and no culture parameter.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/WasmCulture/WasmCultureSetup.cs" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            Optional, and not part of the fix. <code>UseCultureFromServer(this WebApplicationBuilder)</code> is this
            demo's own helper, named after the key it sets; the framework's only surface is the configuration key
            <code>Components:UseCultureFromServer</code>. Because this app calls <code>AddLocalization()</code>, the
            feature is on already, and setting <code>"true"</code> just makes the switch visible. You'd only set the
            key to turn the feature off, or on in an app without localization.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Repeat what the server already knew</h2>
    <p>To keep the two renders in step, .NET 10 apps passed the culture along by hand. The repro tried both common ways:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two .NET 10 workarounds, and what the repro showed</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"
        <span class="hl">autostart="false"</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
  Blazor.start({ webAssembly: {
    <span class="hl">applicationCulture:</span>
      "@@CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.Name" } });
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">a hand-written start-up script in App.razor</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ both lines stayed German</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[Parameter] public string? Culture { get; set; }

@@Total.ToString("N2", <span class="hl">culture</span>)
@@Due.ToString("D")  <span class="err">← forgot it</span></pre>
                <span class="why">the culture's name passed to each component, and handed to every format call</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the total stayed German; the date flipped to the browser's language</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The first works, but it's a start-up script just to repeat what the server already knew. The second fails
        the moment one format call is missed, as Fig. 1's third row shows.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The culture travels with the page</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the server saves its culture and UI culture with the page's persisted state as it prerenders,
        and the WebAssembly host sets them as it starts, before any component renders. No script and no parameter:
        plain formatting gives the same answer on both sides. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Prerendering carries it.</b> With <code>prerender: false</code> there's no server render to take the culture from, so WebAssembly uses the browser's language.</li>
        <li><b>Set once, at start.</b> After an enhanced navigation WebAssembly keeps its culture, which is why the demo's culture links load the page in full.</li>
        <li><b>Globalization data.</b> WebAssembly loads data for the browser's own language group. For a culture outside it, like German in a Japanese browser, set <code>BlazorWebAssemblyLoadAllGlobalizationData</code> to <code>true</code> in the client project.</li>
        <li><b>Turn it off</b> by setting <code>Components:UseCultureFromServer</code> to <code>false</code>. Older posts show a <code>UseCultureFromServer</code> property from Preview 5; it was replaced by this key. (Fig. 5's method of the same name is the demo's own helper, not that property.)</li>
    </ul>
</article>
